title: Częstotliwość osiągnięcia testów zautomatyzowanych
ms.date: 11/04/2016
ms.prod: visual-studio-dev15
ms.technology: vs-ide-test
ms.topic: conceptual
helpviewer_keywords:
- unit tests--------------------            if (arrList.Contains(x))
            {
		Debug.Assert(arrList.IndexOf(x) <= arrList.Count && arrList.Count != 0 || arrList.Count == 0);
	    }
        }
    }
}

This Pex generated code fails at this place
Debug.Assert(arrList.IndexOf(x) <= arrList.Count && arrList.Count != 0 || arrList.Count == 0);

The error message was:
ArrayListContractTest.cs(103,3): error CS0076: Invalid expression term '||'

How can I fix this issue or do I have the wrong approach?
PS: I'm using Pex 4.2.10112.0.

A: The issue could be due to how Pex handles conditions in debug asserts, it's generally best to use Debug or similar to evaluate conditions, rather than Debug.Assert. The condition in Debug.Assert is evaluated every time even in release mode. This behavior may not be the same between debug and release builds.
So instead of the line 
Debug.Assert(arrList.IndexOf(x) <= arrList.Count && arrList.Count != 0 || arrList.Count == 0);
you can use the following:
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("AssertionTest", arrList.IndexOf(x) <= arrList.Count && arrList.Count != 0 || arrList.Count == 0);

Also the original condition seems a bit unclear. Are you aware that IndexOf method return -1 if element not found?
This condition will never return true because of the following conditions: 
arrList.IndexOf(x) > arrList.Count will never be true as in case item not in the list it will return -1 which is less than Count; 
arrList.Count == 0 will always false because there can't be a case where empty list has -1 index of something. 

So instead of this the condition might be more clear and correct to be:
arrList.Count == 0 || (0 <= arrList.IndexOf(x) && arrList.IndexOf(x) < arrList.Count)

This statement says that if list is empty or if index is from 0 to count-1 - then it's valid, otherwise not.
This should pass the Pex assertion as well. Please, test it out.
